BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Decays of beautiful hadrons
DTSTART:20261021T193000Z
DTEND:20261021T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10800@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Federico Cima (Caltech)\n\nIn this talk\, I will pre
 sent some recent progress on some classes of heavy hadron decays. I will p
 resent a comprehensive study of radiative semileptonic b-hadron decays\, s
 emileptonic decays accompanied by an additional photon in the final state.
  Modeling these decays is important for current and future B-factories\, a
 s they constitute an unavoidable background for ordinary semileptonic deca
 ys when the photon goes undetected. Time permitting\, I will also discuss 
 charmless B decays to light pseudo-scalar mesons and how these can be used
  to test the size of U-spin breaking.\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/
 event/10800/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10800/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Strategies for Sub-GeV Dark Matter Searches with Neutrino Obse
 rvatories and Xenon Experiments
DTSTART:20261023T193000Z
DTEND:20261023T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10808@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Lillian Santos-Olmsted (SLAC)\n\nIt was recently sho
 wn that standard sub-GeV dark matter (DM) candidates can be effectively pr
 obed by large neutrino observatories via annual modulation of the total ph
 otomultiplier hit rate. That work focused on the production of light by th
 e excitation of scintillator molecules and considered the JUNO detector\, 
 surpassing limits from dedicated dark-matter detectors and reaching theore
 tical targets. Here\, we significantly generalize that work\, now also tak
 ing into account ionization channels and extending the analysis to other l
 iquid-scintillator detectors\, including SNO+\, Daya Bay\, Borexino\, and 
 KamLAND. We also extend this idea to dual-phase xenon experiments\, using 
 an analogous “dark-electron rate”\, a term we use to refer to the tota
 l rate of raw electron hits. While single-electron searches at low DM mass
 es in XENON1T and XENONnT have strong cuts that keep only 0.01% of the exp
 osure\, we advocate for an approach where instead xenon detectors should u
 se the bulk of the dark-electron rate and perform an annual-modulation sea
 rch. This technique can also be used to probe DM-nucleon interactions via 
 the Migdal effect.\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10808/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10808/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Witten effect for global monopoles
DTSTART:20261028T193000Z
DTEND:20261028T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10809@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Pablo Quilez (CERN)\n\nGlobal monopoles can carry el
 ectric charge even when the scalar fields that form them are neutral. I wi
 ll show how an interaction usually discarded as a total derivative produce
 s a Witten effect for these global defects\, giving them an electric charg
 e proportional to their winding number. I will explain its origin and illu
 strate the mechanism in a simple UV completion.\nBased on arXiv:2610.14168
  [hep-ph]\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10809/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10809/
END:VEVENT
