BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Diffraction from first principles
DTSTART:20261104T203000Z
DTEND:20261104T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10825@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Stella Schindler (LANL)\n\nDiffractive processes com
 prise nearly half the cross section at the LHC\, and are a key probe of ph
 enomena ranging from Regge (forward) physics\, to the behavior of cosmic r
 ay showers\, to the saturation of nuclear matter. Diffractive processes ex
 hibit unique properties like forward scattering and large “rapidity gaps
 ” of the detector devoid of particles that have so far defied a systemat
 ic field theory description. We use effective field theory techniques to d
 escribe diffraction from first principles\, opening up new frontiers for e
 xploration at the dawn of the EIC\, HL-LHC\, and FAIR accelerators.\n\nhtt
 ps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10825/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10825/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Magnus expansion and its classical limit
DTSTART:20261106T203000Z
DTEND:20261106T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10826@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Graham Brown (U Edinburgh)\n\nThe Magnus expansion h
 as recently emerged as an alternative to the conventional Dyson expansion 
 in QFT. In the Magnus expansion\, we perturbatively expand the logarithm o
 f the S-matrix. The matrix elements of this logarithm are Magnus amplitude
 s\, which have many pleasing properties\, including a well-behaved classic
 al limit. In this talk we will introduce the Magnus expansion and explain 
 its diagrammatic formulation.  Then\, we will study the classical limit o
 f certain Magnus amplitudes and show explicitly that they are free of clas
 sically singular terms. Our analysis will rely on an algebra of permutatio
 ns\, which we will also use to derive new closed-form expressions for Muru
 a coefficients at any loop order. Based on: 2512.05017 and 2609.40291.\n\n
 https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10826/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10826/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Axion Interactions with Topological Defects
DTSTART:20261111T203000Z
DTEND:20261111T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10827@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Katie Fraser (UC Berkeley)\n\n \nIn this talk\, we 
 explore how the compact field space of axions shapes their interactions wi
 th topological defects. We first show how virtual monopole loops generate 
 an axion potential through the Witten effect\, including discussing the th
 eories with fermions\, and give an example in which this potential dominat
 es the axion mass. We then turn to superconducting axion strings\, discuss
 ing the impact of an explicit PQ breaking mass term on the superconducting
  zero modes which live on the string.\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/
 event/10827/
LOCATION:48/2-224 - Madrone (SLAC)
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10827/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Dark Dimension\, Revisited
DTSTART:20261113T203000Z
DTEND:20261113T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T012600Z
UID:indico-event-10838@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Vincent Lee (UC Berkeley)\n\nMotivated by the swampl
 and program\, it has been proposed that a tower of massive Kaluza-Klein (K
 K) gravitons from a compactified flat extra dimension can be dark matter\,
  with the heavier modes constantly cascading to lighter ones. The phenomen
 ology crucially depends on the rate at which a massive KK mode decays to l
 ighter modes. We show that\, up to mild assumptions on the UV theory\, the
  decay rate is suppressed by a factor of (q/m)^4 compared to the one commo
 nly used in the literature\, where q is the momentum of the decay produc
 ts and m is the mass of the decaying KK mode. This suggests that the prop
 osed scenario is ruled out by CMB observations by many orders of magnitude
 . Remarkably\, the same suppression opens up a vastly different parameter 
 space in which KK gravitons from a strongly warped extra dimension (e.g. a
  Randall-Sundrum setup) are a viable dark matter candidate\, either as the
  cascading gravitons themselves or as a stable component of the tower.\n\n
 https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10838/
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10838/
END:VEVENT
